\section{Hosting these C utilities on SBCL}

This file is the clearest illustration of why the GCL binding cannot be
carried verbatim to SBCL, and of the right response.

The four utilities Axiom needs here --- \verb|directoryp|,
\verb|writeablep|, \verb|hashString| and \verb|hashCombine| --- are bound
under GCL with \verb|defentry|, and the two hash functions supply their C
bodies inline with \verb|Clines|. \verb|Clines| asks the host Lisp to
compile a snippet of C \emph{into its own image}. GCL, descended from a
Lisp that was half a C compiler, can do this. SBCL cannot: it has no
facility to compile C into the running image, and its foreign interface,
\verb|sb-alien|, can only call functions that already live in a loaded
shared object.

One could therefore build a small shared library and reach it with
\verb|sb-alien:define-alien-routine|. For \emph{these} functions that
would be effort spent badly, because each is trivial: the two hashes are
a handful of integer operations, and the two filesystem predicates are
already written in portable Lisp in the \verb|#+:CCL| branch below. The
right SBCL port here is not foreign at all --- we reimplement the four in
pure Common Lisp. This removes a foreign dependency and makes the
behaviour visible in this book instead of hidden in a C string.

Real foreign calls are still required elsewhere: the seventeen socket
entries in \verb|sockio.lisp.pamphlet| genuinely cross into C, and those
are handled with \verb|sb-alien| or stubbed when the HyperDoc server is
not built. The rule the whole port follows: \emph{reimplement trivial C
in Lisp; reserve \texttt{sb-alien} for code that is irreducibly foreign.}

The pure-Lisp hashes must reproduce the C bit-for-bit, because hash
values may be stored and compared across sessions. \verb|MYHASH|
exclusive-ors the running value left-shifted by eight, adds the
character code plus a constant, and masks to thirty bits;
\verb|MYCOMBINE| shifts the masked second argument left by six, adds the
first, and reduces modulo a prime. Both are transcribed below. The
filesystem predicates are less exact: \verb|directoryp| follows the
\verb|#+:CCL| precedent, while \verb|writeablep| is a non-creating
open-probe whose correspondence to the C \verb|access(2)| test ---
particularly on directories --- is flagged for verification in Stage~5.

\begin{chunk}{*}


(in-package "BOOT")

#+(and :Lucid (not :ibm/370))
(progn
; these appear to be unused in axiom code
;  (system:define-foreign-function :c '|findString| :fixnum)
;  (system:define-foreign-function :c '|copyEnvValue| :fixnum)
  (system:define-foreign-function :c '|addtopath|  :fixnum)
  (system:define-foreign-function :c '|chdir|      :fixnum)
  (system:define-foreign-function :c '|writeablep| :fixnum)
  (system:define-foreign-function :c '|directoryp| :fixnum)
  )

#+KCL
(progn
; these appear to be unused in axiom code
;  (defentry |findString| (string string) (int "findString"))
  (defentry |directoryp| (string)        (int "directoryp"))
  (defentry |writeablep| (string)        (int "writeablep"))
  )

#+:CCL
(defun |directoryp| (fn)
  (cond ((not (probe-file fn)) -1)
        ((directoryp fn) 1)
        (t 0)))

#+:sbcl
(defun |directoryp| (fn)
  "1 if FN names a directory, 0 if a non-directory file, -1 if absent.
   A directory truename has neither a name nor a type component."
  (let ((truename (probe-file fn)))
    (cond ((null truename) -1)
          ((and (null (pathname-name truename))
                (null (pathname-type truename))) 1)
          (t 0))))

#+:sbcl
(defun |writeablep| (fn)
  "1 if FN is writable, -1 otherwise. GCL's C writeablep used access(W_OK),
   which works for directories; an open(:output) probe does not -- you
   cannot open a directory for output. myWritable?/fnameNew call this on
   the *directory* that will hold a new file, so dispatch: probe a
   directory by creating+deleting a temp file in it; probe a regular file
   with a non-creating open-append."
  (if (eql 1 (|directoryp| fn))
      (let ((probe (merge-pathnames
                    (make-pathname :name (symbol-name (gensym "AXWTEST"))
                                   :type "tmp")
                    (truename fn))))
        (handler-case
            (progn
              (with-open-file (s probe :direction :output
                                       :if-does-not-exist :create)
                (declare (ignore s)))
              (ignore-errors (delete-file probe))
              1)
          (file-error () -1)))
      (handler-case
          (with-open-file (s fn :direction :output
                                :if-exists :append
                                :if-does-not-exist nil)
            (if s 1 -1))
        (file-error () -1))))

(defun |findStringInFile| (str p)
    (|findString| (namestring p) str) )

#+:gcl
(defun |getEnv| (var-name)  (system::getenv var-name))
#+:sbcl
(defun |getEnv| (var-name)  (sb-ext:posix-getenv var-name))

;;; Portable chdir — GCL has system::chdir built in; SBCL uses alien call.
#+:sbcl
(defun system::chdir (dir)
  "Change process working directory.  GCL compatibility shim for SBCL.
SBCL's truename/directory use *default-pathname-defaults*, not the
OS CWD, so we must update both."
  (require 'sb-posix)
  (funcall (symbol-function (intern "CHDIR" "SB-POSIX")) dir)
  ;; Read the new CWD back from the kernel rather than (truename dir): a
  ;; relative DIR would otherwise be resolved against the not-yet-updated
  ;; *default-pathname-defaults*, desynchronising the two.
  (setq *default-pathname-defaults*
        (pathname (concatenate 'string
                    (funcall (symbol-function (intern "GETCWD" "SB-POSIX")))
                    "/"))))

;;stolen from AXIOM-XL src/strops.c
#+(AND KCL (NOT ELF))
(Clines 
"MYHASH(s)"
"char *s;"
"{"
" register unsigned int   h = 0;"
" register int    c;"
""
" while ((c = *s++) != 0) {"
"  h ^= (h << 8);"
"  h += ((c) + 200041);"
"  h &= 0x3FFFFFFF;"
" }"
" return h;"
"}"
)
#+(AND KCL (NOT ELF))
(defentry |hashString| (string) (int "MYHASH"))
#+(AND KCL ELF)
(defun |hashString| (string) (system:|hashString| string))
#+:sbcl
(defun |hashString| (s)
  "Pure-Lisp transcription of the C MYHASH; must match it bit-for-bit."
  (let ((h 0))
    (loop for c across s do
      (setf h (logand (+ (logxor h (ash h 8)) (char-code c) 200041)
                      #x3FFFFFFF)))
    h))

\end{chunk}
The MYCOMBINE function causes a problem as of GCL-2.6.8pre.
Camm has rewritten it. This used to read:
\begin{verbatim}
#+(AND KCL (NOT ELF))
(Clines
"unsigned int MYCOMBINE(i,j)"
"unsigned int i,j;"
"{"
"return ( (((j & 16777215) @<< 6)+i) % 1073741789);"
"}"
)
\end{verbatim}
\begin{chunk}{*}
#+(AND KCL (NOT ELF))
(Clines
"int MYCOMBINE(i,j)"
"int i,j;"
"{"
"return ( (((((unsigned int)j) & 16777215) << 6)+((unsigned int)i)) % 1073741789);"
"}"
)


#+(AND KCL (NOT ELF))
(defentry |hashCombine| (int int) (int "MYCOMBINE"))
#+(AND KCL  ELF)
(defun |hashCombine| (x y) (system:|hashCombine| x y))
#+:sbcl
(defun |hashCombine| (i j)
  "Pure-Lisp transcription of the C MYCOMBINE; must match it bit-for-bit."
  (mod (+ (ash (logand j 16777215) 6) i) 1073741789))


\end{chunk}
